WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.000
- Good afternoon everybody,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.170
Peter Salovey, President
Peter Salovey and all.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.340
Welcome to the second of this year's

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.150
Tanner lectures on human values

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.590
by Achille Mbembe, professor at the

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.620
University of the
Witwatersrand in Johannesburg

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.390
and at the Wits Institute
for Social and Economic

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:26.993
Research at that University.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.060
Quick introduction today,
we don't stand on too much

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.890
formality on the second
day of the Tanners.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.290
Remember please that
we will reconvene here

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.430
tomorrow morning at
10:30 for the final event

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.020
of this year's Tanners and
that is a panel discussion

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.250
among Professor Mbembe and Yale professors

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.593
Inderpal Grewal and Michael Denning.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.160
I was in a funny conversation
a couple of days ago

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.140
with Achille and Michael about those

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.700
occasions where you give a talk and then

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.010
someone stands up afterwards to

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.180
summarize it and you think to yourself

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.630
did I really talk about that?

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.242
So I won't today take
the risk of summarizing

00:01:05.242 --> 00:01:06.820
Achille's lecture from yesterday

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.030
but only signal a few of his themes,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.400
remind you of a few of its themes

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.430
and hope that he might
recognize the signals

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.330
as something he did indeed talk about.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.520
We learned yesterday
about the new mobility

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.953
regime of the lectures
title and its implications

00:01:20.953 --> 00:01:23.120
for the changing nature of borders

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.320
and their effects in our world.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.370
We heard about the border as a zone of

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.420
securitization and control

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.720
and about three different
approaches to theorizing

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.130
this zone, approaches that characterized

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.670
those against whom the border is mobilized

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.760
in terms of precarity,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.120
in terms of extreme vulnerability,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.809
of detention, incarceration, violence.

00:01:44.809 --> 00:01:48.390
But we also had a glimpse
of something more hopeful,

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.710
a border as a place even of rescue

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.620
and we heard finally, fascinatingly

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.530
and at some length about
an existential flip

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.150
by which the features of
borders at land and sea

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.620
have been exchanged.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.880
The fluidity of sea borders

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.470
described, theorized, made almost mythic

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.926
by Carl Schmitt has now been transferred

00:02:08.926 --> 00:02:11.200
to land borders.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.270
This has happened because we
live in that mobility regime

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.750
of Achille's title.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.240
The medium of land
borders is no longer land

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.040
but has become a fluid medium

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.400
and what does this medium consist of?

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.930
The tens of millions of

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.890
displaced human bodies
defining new border zones

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.720
by their movements, zones then
not limited to liminal spaces

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.843
but extending everywhere
those movements reach.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.090
This might also look like
a hopeful development

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.580
and perhaps we will see how
it is so in today's lecture

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.960
entitled The Idea of a Borderless World.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.900
But not so fast, because Achille was quick

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.100
to remind us that in the
era of digitized biometrics

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.000
and securitized borders
even the fluid motions

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.010
of millions can be tracked and controlled.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:55.783
We will find out, Achille.

00:02:56.968 --> 00:02:59.968
(audience applauds)

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.890
- Thank you very much Gary.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.940
I indeed recognize myself

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.410
in the brilliant summary

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.670
of this lecture you have just given us.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.810
I would also like to thank

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.553
the president of the university, Peter,

00:03:27.553 --> 00:03:30.170
who is with us this evening.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.330
Yesterday I intimated that

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.060
the government of mobility

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:38.990
might well be

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.620
one of the most important problems

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.770
to confront the world
during the first half

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.593
of the 21st century.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.543
The capacity to decide who can move,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:54.667
who can settle,

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.070
where and under what conditions

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.830
is increasingly becoming

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.750
the core of political struggles

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.650
over sovereignty,

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:12.200
over nationalism, citizenship, identity,

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.113
security and freedom.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.060
I strongly suggested that with Western

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.340
colonial expansion and more decisively

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.563
with the advent of capitalism,

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.830
the raison d'etre of the border

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:35.810
is to attend to key questions

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:39.433
such as to whom does the earth belong,

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.930
who can lay what types of claims

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.243
to what parts of it,

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.770
and to raise beings who inhabit them,

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.873
who determines its
distribution or partition?

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.760
In framing the question
of the border in this way

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.790
I was trying to show that the power

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:07.520
of the border lies

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.290
in its capacity to regulate

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.540
what are called the multiple distributions

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.160
of populations and by population I mean

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.173
humans and non-humans.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.890
Their distribution on
the body of the earth

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:28.610
and in so doing to affect the vital forces

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.223
of all kinds of beings.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.000
I also argued that as
the 21st century unfolds

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:41.740
a global renewed desire from both citizens

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.820
and their respective states
for a tighter control

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.833
of mobility is evident.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.960
Indeed wherever we look, the
drive is towards inclusion

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.590
or in any case towards an

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.610
intensification of the dialectics of

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:06.150
territorialization and
deterritorialization,

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.040
the dialectics of opening and inclusion

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.730
The belief that the world would be safer

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.780
if only risks ambiguity and uncertainty

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.497
could be controlled and if only identities

00:06:21.497 --> 00:06:25.673
could be fixed once for all and secured,

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.570
that belief is gaining momentum

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.180
and risk management techniques

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.170
are increasingly becoming a means

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:39.833
to govern mobilities.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.193
Such techniques are indeed proliferating.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.190
In particular

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.813
I highlighted the extent to which,

00:06:51.813 --> 00:06:55.230
as Gary was reminding
us, the biometric border

00:06:55.230 --> 00:07:00.050
is extending into multiple realms

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.770
not only of social life but of the body

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:08.573
that body are called the
body that is not mine.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.680
Tonight, I would like to pursue

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:17.090
this line of argument concerning the

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.880
redistribution of the earth

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:24.280
not only through the
control of bodies but also

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.590
through the control of movement itself

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:31.590
and its corollary speed which indeed is

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:36.120
what migration control
policies are all about,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.660
controlling bodies but
also movement itself

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.453
and its corollary speed.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.820
More specifically I would
like to see whether,

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:51.540
and under what conditions,
we could re-engineer

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.150
the utopia of a borderless Africa

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.220
and by extension a borderless world

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.300
since as far as I know
Africa is part of the world

00:08:06.175 --> 00:08:08.663
and the world is part of Africa.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:15.010
I would like to do that,
taking into consideration

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:18.680
the contemporary matrix of flows, closures

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.260
and circulations including the circulation

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.033
of various forms of capital.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:29.880
Now you will ask me
but why is it important

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.780
to attend once again to

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:36.030
what is obviously a utopian intent,

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.003
the question of a borderless world?

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:43.333
I think it's important
for a number of reasons.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:48.900
The first which is, let's
say, a philosophical reason

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:52.750
is that from its inception

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:59.220
movement or more precisely borderlessness

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:04.450
has been central to
various utopian traditions.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.830
When you look at the
very concept of utopia

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.930
that is what it refers to,

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:14.200
it refers to that which has no borders,

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.813
beginning with the imagination itself.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.190
I think the power of utopianism

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:25.610
resides in its ability to instantiate

00:09:26.644 --> 00:09:30.900
the tension between borderlessness,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.050
movement and place.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:36.700
A tension, if we look carefully, that has

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:40.223
marked social transformations
in the modern era.

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:48.450
This tension continues in
contemporary discussions

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:53.580
of movement based social processes

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.993
particularly international migration,

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.220
open borders, transnationalism

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.330
and even cosmopolitanism.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.460
When you read Kant on cosmopolitanism

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.075
Kant's discourse on cosmopolitanism is

00:10:11.075 --> 00:10:16.075
traversed from one end to
the other by this tension

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:20.483
between borderlessness,
place and movement.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:25.430
So it seems to me that in this context

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.200
the idea of a borderless
world can be a powerful

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:33.900
albeit problematic resource for

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.383
social, political and even
aesthetic imagination.

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:41.880
The second reason is that

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.430
precisely because of the current atrophy

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:50.103
of the utopian imagination,

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:54.426
apocalyptic imageries and narratives

00:10:54.426 --> 00:10:57.998
of cataclysmic disasters
and unknown futures

00:10:57.998 --> 00:11:02.890
have colonized the spirit of our times.

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:04.090
We find them everywhere,

00:11:05.971 --> 00:11:09.890
in popular culture, in
political discourses, in cinema,

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.213
in music, they're everywhere.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:19.340
Indeed, some of the ecological anxieties

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:22.800
and fantasies of the apocalypse

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.610
I would argue are the
product of the Anthropocene

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:32.700
but what politics do visions
of future catastrophe

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:36.570
engender if not a politics of separation

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:41.040
rather than a politics of the humanity

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.883
as species coming into being
as stewards of the earth?

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.130
Because we inherited history in which

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:54.330
the consistent sacrifice of some lives

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.580
for the betterment of others is the norm

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:03.910
and because these are times
of deep-seated anxieties

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:08.910
including anxieties of racialized others

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.483
taking over the planet,

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:12.920
because of all of that

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:18.040
racial violence is increasingly encoded

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:20.120
in the language of the border

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:21.720
and in the language of security.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:28.550
As a result, contemporary
borders are in danger

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:32.270
of becoming sites of reinforcement,

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:37.270
reproduction and
intensification of vulnerability

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:42.050
for stigmatized and dishonored groups,

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:47.050
for the most racially marked,
the ever more disposable,

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:52.030
those that in this era of
a neoliberal abandonment

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:56.640
have been paying the heaviest of prices

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.750
for the most expensive period

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:02.373
of prison construction in human history.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:09.730
Refer to the prison here
precisely to the casual landscapes

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:11.560
of a world

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:16.590
precisely as the antithesis of movement,

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:17.853
of freedom of movement.

00:13:19.467 --> 00:13:22.710
Is no dramatic opposite

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:27.210
of the idea of freedom
of movement than prison

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.760
and the prison is

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:33.280
key feature

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:36.973
of the landscapes of our times.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:44.210
So in proposing to reexamine

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.840
the question of a borderless Africa

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:47.940
and a borderless world

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:54.180
I would like to stay away
from dominant ways in which

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.360
this issue has easily been dealt with.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:01.640
That is, under the sign of Kant

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:03.890
and his promise of

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.483
unbounded cosmopolitanism,

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:15.520
and I would likely stay away from

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.180
the other dominant
approach to the question

00:14:20.064 --> 00:14:21.164
of a borderless world,

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.640
one under the sign of
liberal individualism

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:30.410
understood as an antidote

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:36.830
or to the deeply
ingrained fascist impulses

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:39.503
of European governments and bureaucracies.

00:14:41.131 --> 00:14:43.940
Referring yesterday at dinner with Gary

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:47.980
to a very important chapter in

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:53.230
Arendt's important classic book,

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:54.973
The Origins of Totalitarianism.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:59.003
As chapter two it's called
Race and Bureaucracy.

00:15:01.782 --> 00:15:03.282
The reference I have just made

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:07.380
alludes to indeed that chapter,

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:09.730
which as I was signaling

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:15.550
for some unknown reasons
has not really been

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:16.383
the object

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.853
of major consideration.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:25.010
So for those of you who
are looking for a topic

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:29.090
for your dissertation
there's one right there.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:32.083
Chapter two, The Origins
of Totalitarianism.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:39.340
So I would like to stay
away from these two modes of

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.653
approaching the question
of a borderless world.

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.220
Both of them, in fact,

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:53.310
though they seem to be worlds apart

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.360
have something in common.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:58.290
They both

00:16:01.423 --> 00:16:06.160
articulated around a concept

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.980
you might hopefully know of,

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.043
the concept of the fourth freedom.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:16.570
In classical liberal thought

00:16:20.078 --> 00:16:21.528
there are three core freedoms

00:16:24.509 --> 00:16:26.062
First of all

00:16:26.062 --> 00:16:27.729
freedom of movement,

00:16:31.747 --> 00:16:34.120
and within freedom of movement,

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.860
movement of of capital of course,

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:38.460
priority number one

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:44.370
cause there is no capital without goods.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:46.150
The freedom of movement of goods

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:51.680
and more and more especially
in these times of ours,

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.900
number three, services,

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:58.210
freedom, movement of those
who can provide services.

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:00.000
Those are the three freedoms,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.733
the three core freedoms.

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:07.040
So the concept of the fourth freedom

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:10.303
has to do with the movement of persons.

00:17:14.493 --> 00:17:17.470
So traditional engagements

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.460
with the idea of a borderless world

00:17:22.176 --> 00:17:25.810
aim at precipitating

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:28.170
the advent of that fourth freedom

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:30.880
which is freedom of movement of persons

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:37.110
and within that configuration

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:42.520
a borderless world would
look something like this.

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:46.720
It would be a world of free movement,

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:51.720
of capital, of goods, services and persons

00:17:56.444 --> 00:17:59.530
and such movement, such
freedom of movement

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:02.820
would not be restricted to the core,

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:06.960
economically rich countries or states,

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:08.763
which is the case as we speak.

00:18:10.839 --> 00:18:15.100
That the Schengen system, for instance,

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:17.873
is limited to the core European countries.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:23.240
In fact, if you have an

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:25.700
American passport you can basically go

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:26.643
wherever you want.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:29.553
The world belongs to you.

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:32.813
But this is not the case for

00:18:32.813 --> 00:18:35.543
every inhabitant of our planet.

00:18:37.267 --> 00:18:40.380
So in the configuration

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.240
I have just referred to,

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.343
the fourth freedom,

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:50.190
the ability to move around the planet

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:53.320
would no longer limited,
to put it crudely,

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:55.733
to Europeans and to Americans.

00:18:56.886 --> 00:19:01.830
It would be a radical right
that would belong to everybody

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:06.030
by virtue of each and every individual

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:07.063
being a human being.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:15.170
So it's a right that would be

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:20.170
extended including to
poor members of the earth.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:23.793
So you see we go back to
this question of the earth.

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:27.550
There would be no visas,

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:31.800
meaning in some let's say instances

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:34.400
of the fourth freedom movement

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.290
there would be no visas,

00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:38.103
there would be no quotas,

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:42.480
there wouldn't be any
bizarre category to fill.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.780
First of all because you
wouldn't have to apply

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:48.333
for a visa so wouldn't
have to fill anything.

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:53.570
There would be no application

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.547
procedures preceding any move

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:03.040
which means that one
could just get on a plane,

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:06.000
on a train, on a boat, on the road,

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:11.000
on a bike, rights of non-discrimination

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:12.993
would be extended to all.

00:20:14.831 --> 00:20:16.310
So a borderless world

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:18.510
would be the world in which

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:23.610
circulatory and pendulum migrations

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:27.390
would be the norm with in fact longer term

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:30.520
settlements less likely since borders

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:32.263
would be easy and open.

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:38.750
I will give you one little example

00:20:40.597 --> 00:20:41.880
please when I was growing up,

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:43.720
I mean not so long ago,

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:45.630
don't don't believe Gary when he says

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:47.363
we met 25 years ago.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:51.983
We did but we were both very, very young.

00:20:51.983 --> 00:20:54.064
(audience laughs)

00:20:54.064 --> 00:20:57.470
So not so long ago I was born in Cameroon.

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.090
Cameroon used to be a German protectorate

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:04.750
which became after the First World War

00:21:06.535 --> 00:21:09.450
divided between the

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:14.260
winning powers, England on the one hand

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:16.104
and France on the other.

00:21:16.104 --> 00:21:18.154
I was born in the French part of Cameroon

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:23.340
and until the beginning of the 80s

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.560
it was possible to travel to France

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.150
with one's identity card,

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:29.543
national identity card.

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:37.803
Most people went to France and came back.

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:42.520
They didn't go there because
they wanted to settle there.

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:45.480
Most people want to
live where they belong,

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:46.313
quote unquote,

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:50.060
but they want to be able to come and go

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:54.310
and they are more likely to come and go

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:58.513
when the borders are
not hermetically closed.

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:02.630
So the borderless world imagined by

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:04.200
the fourth freedom movement

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:10.270
is premised therefore on these
rights of non-discrimination

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.400
and on this circulatory and pendular

00:22:16.286 --> 00:22:17.363
set of migrations.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:23.450
Now let me move on

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:28.400
I wanna go further into

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:29.660
those debates,

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:34.660
they are both complicated
and quite straightforward.

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:39.110
We have those who support

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.760
free movement of labor and those who

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:47.760
believe that it only intensifies
levels of exploitation,

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:51.682
especially of migrants,
we can talk about it

00:22:51.682 --> 00:22:54.110
during the discussions.

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:56.880
What I really want to do now

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:01.221
is to still in this attempt at

00:23:01.221 --> 00:23:04.690
elucidating or posing differently

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:07.050
the question of a borderless world,

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:09.700
what I would like to do is to

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:12.763
contrast two paradigms.

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:15.950
On the one hand

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:23.360
examine the liberal idea
of a borderless world

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:25.860
through the

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:28.680
free movement

00:23:31.149 --> 00:23:32.049
let's say concept.

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:35.730
So contrast it with African pre-colonial

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:38.873
understandings of movement in space.

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.140
That's what I want to do

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:44.990
in the hope that in contrasting these

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:47.610
two paradigms we might begin to

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:50.140
let's say this will

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:55.820
give us conceptual resources to expand

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:58.553
on this utopian project,

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:02.040
project of a borderless world.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.950
When I say liberal classical thought

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:09.810
of course it is extremely complicated,

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:10.860
you understand that,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.240
I don't have the time to go into

00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:16.120
all the nuances, I'm giving you a kind of

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:19.060
archetype which needs to be itself

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:21.213
properly deconstructed.

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:26.450
And here I will rely in particular

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:29.230
on a recently published work called

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:33.270
Movement and the Ordering of Freedom.

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:36.480
Just keep in mind that idea
of the ordering of freedom.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:41.100
It's a work has been published by

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:45.907
Hagar Kotef, an Israeli
scholar who teaches

00:24:45.907 --> 00:24:50.470
at the School of Oriental
and African Studies in London

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:56.320
and you might

00:24:58.367 --> 00:25:01.187
let your imagination work and understand

00:25:02.048 --> 00:25:04.002
why it is that it's an Israeli who is

00:25:04.002 --> 00:25:06.063
let's say interested in this.

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:13.920
So what Hagar shows in that work

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:18.040
is the extent to which liberal thought,

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:22.020
liberal political thought, has in fact

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:24.240
always been saddled with a

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:28.030
contradiction when it comes to imagining

00:25:28.030 --> 00:25:31.113
the possibility of a borderless world.

00:25:32.650 --> 00:25:35.090
And her argument is that

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:40.190
this contradiction which
I will elicit in a moment

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:43.883
stems from its conception of movement.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:50.830
She shows that, in fact,

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:54.940
two dominant configurations of movement

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:58.943
constantly come into
conflict with each other,

00:26:00.429 --> 00:26:01.780
the terms cancel each other,

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:04.463
within classical liberal thought.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:07.870
Movement here

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:12.870
is seen both as a manifestation of freedom

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:17.040
and as an interruption of order,

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:18.373
as a threat to order,

00:26:23.099 --> 00:26:25.010
and one of the functions of the state

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:26.223
is therefore,

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:30.510
to craft a concept of order

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:33.480
and stability

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:38.470
and security that is reconcilable

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:42.280
with its concept of freedom

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:43.843
and its concept of movement.

00:26:45.150 --> 00:26:46.800
That is the the contradiction

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:55.403
and to some extent, Hagar argues,

00:26:57.050 --> 00:27:02.050
the liberal classical state is the enemy

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:06.803
of people who restlessly move around.

00:27:09.821 --> 00:27:14.821
Why, because such people are configured

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:19.780
as an in-assimilable authority,

00:27:19.780 --> 00:27:21.223
you cannot assimilate them.

00:27:22.438 --> 00:27:24.880
They are constantly on the move

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:29.880
and in fact there are career
repercussions of all of this.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:36.170
I mean when colonialism sets
foot in Africa for instance,

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:41.570
the biggest problem of the colonial state

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:46.370
in the continent of Africa
from the 19th century on

00:27:48.230 --> 00:27:51.863
is to make sure that people
stay in the same place.

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:55.120
It has a hard time

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:58.790
bringing them to stay in the same place

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:02.370
because the state believes
that they are constantly

00:28:02.370 --> 00:28:03.203
on the move.

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:08.180
They are, quote unquote, uncaptured

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:11.602
so the business of a state is

00:28:11.602 --> 00:28:16.130
how to capture them because why
do you want to capture them?

00:28:16.130 --> 00:28:18.830
You want to capture them precisely because

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:27.660
without capturing them
sovereignty doesn't mean anything.

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:30.790
Sovereignty means you capture people,

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:35.080
you capture a territory,
you delimited borders

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:39.560
and this allows you in turn to exercise

00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:44.418
the different types of
monopolies Max Weber spoke about,

00:28:44.418 --> 00:28:46.690
a monopoly over of course territory,

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:48.560
a monopoly over the people,

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:52.900
monopoly in terms of the
use of legitimate force

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:56.153
and very importantly
because everything else

00:28:56.153 --> 00:28:59.053
depends on that monopoly over taxation.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:03.278
But you can't tax people

00:29:03.278 --> 00:29:05.583
who have no address,

00:29:06.644 --> 00:29:07.545
today here,

00:29:07.545 --> 00:29:12.545
tomorrow morning they're
going for (mumbles)

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:16.730
and next week they have moved

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:19.093
to a different entirely location.

00:29:21.335 --> 00:29:25.430
So the state sees such people as enemies

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:29.440
both of freedom because
they abuse freedom,

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:33.010
they don't exercise it with restraint

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:36.213
and enemies of security and order.

00:29:36.213 --> 00:29:38.071
You can't build an order on the basis of

00:29:38.071 --> 00:29:39.893
that which is unstable.

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:45.420
So that's a very important aspect

00:29:46.641 --> 00:29:51.641
of, let's say, what
classical liberal theory

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:55.543
tells us about movement.

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:02.810
The same state is a friend

00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:04.673
of self regulated movement.

00:30:07.100 --> 00:30:11.880
Why, because freedom is here understood

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:15.563
as being about moderation.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.000
It's about self-regulation,

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:20.573
it's not about excess.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:27.570
Excessive movement immediately
conjures problems of security

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:30.760
and bad movement is seen as a threat

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:35.760
and I quote Hagar, "freedom
is only politically valuable

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:39.390
if it relies on some mechanisms

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:42.627
that regulate the movement
that manifests it."

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:46.373
I quote her quoting Hobbes.

00:30:50.635 --> 00:30:54.340
So as Kotef argues,

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:57.390
movement not only had to be restrained

00:30:57.390 --> 00:31:02.110
via an array of disciplinary mechanisms,

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:04.650
it had to be reconciled with freedom

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:07.500
and to some extent self restraint,

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:10.070
but the ability to restrain oneself

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:14.821
or to regulate oneself was not assumed

00:31:14.821 --> 00:31:17.800
to be the share of all subjects.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.060
That is not everybody who is able to

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:21.890
restrain himself or herself

00:31:29.128 --> 00:31:32.177
and some movements were
therefore configured as

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:38.013
freedom and others were deemed improper,

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:41.276
and were conceived as a threat.

00:31:41.276 --> 00:31:44.540
That's the bifurcation we have
in classical liberal thought

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:49.320
and what point is it that
a specific kind of movement

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:54.033
by specific kinds of subjects
is or is not a threat.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:57.550
That's the spectrum that haunts

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:02.540
classical liberal states from those years

00:32:02.540 --> 00:32:04.200
up to now.

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:06.123
We haven't got rid of that spectrum.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:14.130
So the way in which
classical liberal states

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:19.130
have tried to resolve this contradiction

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:24.140
has been by putting forward
the idea of managed mobility

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:27.530
which, by the way, is back on the agenda.

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:31.010
Right now as I speak, in Europe

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:34.790
and even in South Africa
I've been doing some work

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:38.740
with the Department of Home Affairs

00:32:40.665 --> 00:32:45.500
on recalibrating inter African migrations.

00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:48.803
The key concept is managed mobility.

00:32:52.309 --> 00:32:54.570
So within the framework
of managed mobility

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:59.953
and because of the perceived
aberrant movements,

00:33:01.627 --> 00:33:03.427
certain categories of the population

00:33:05.137 --> 00:33:08.030
are constantly seen as posing a threat

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:10.410
not only to themselves,

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.450
to their own security but also to others,

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:18.210
other security and such a threat,

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:21.580
it is thought, can be diminished

00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:23.130
if their movements are confined

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:26.240
and if they are domesticated

00:33:26.240 --> 00:33:29.563
and subject to some form of other reform.

00:33:31.751 --> 00:33:33.753
And this is not new in fact.

00:33:35.470 --> 00:33:38.820
This has been typical indeed

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:43.820
of the ways in which
different figures of mobility

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:48.970
historically have become the foundation

00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:52.220
upon which different modes of governance

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:53.793
were produced.

00:33:57.841 --> 00:34:00.703
In the West, generally taken,

00:34:07.189 --> 00:34:09.430
one such board of governance

00:34:09.430 --> 00:34:14.430
has consisted in
instituting what is called

00:34:15.065 --> 00:34:20.065
a racial border, that
you have physical borders

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:23.993
but you also have a racial border.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:28.980
Such was the case under colonialism,

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:32.210
such was the case of
course in slave owning

00:34:32.210 --> 00:34:34.223
and settler colonial societies.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:40.130
In all those places where settler liberty

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:42.780
and freedom of movement required

00:34:43.866 --> 00:34:46.800
the criminalization of countless others

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:52.580
and such instances of criminalization

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:55.530
and stigmatization became indispensable

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:58.073
features of liberal government.

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:04.140
I could go on and on on that

00:35:07.808 --> 00:35:10.140
but let me move on.

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:12.933
We can pick it up during the discussion.

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:19.810
Let me just capture what I have tried

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:23.430
to say in one or two sentences

00:35:24.323 --> 00:35:26.910
just for you to be able to keep in mind

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:28.770
because now I'll be moving on

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:30.770
and contrasting this model

00:35:31.904 --> 00:35:34.200
with the African pre-colonial model.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:39.200
So in the classical liberal
model I was referring to,

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:41.680
security and freedom

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:46.656
precisely because of the racial border,

00:35:46.656 --> 00:35:49.740
which some of us are
particularly interested in,

00:35:49.740 --> 00:35:53.660
security and freedom came to be defined

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:55.653
as a right of exclusion.

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:01.690
Order within that model

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:06.800
is about securing the

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:11.433
unequal ordering of property
relations, that's what it is,

00:36:13.330 --> 00:36:17.470
and asserting the boundaries of the nation

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:21.670
goes hand-in-hand in that model

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:25.513
with the assertion of
the boundaries of race.

00:36:27.236 --> 00:36:29.620
Now defining the boundaries of race

00:36:29.620 --> 00:36:34.530
within that model requires
a proper definition

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.200
of the boundaries of the body,

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:39.550
as I was arguing is the,

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:42.260
thus the centrality of the body

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:47.023
in the calculus of both
freedom and security.

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:52.563
Now,

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:57.970
let's look into

00:36:57.970 --> 00:37:00.273
what are called the African model.

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:09.973
Here again it's a very
complex consolation.

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:13.870
For lack of time I will go straight to

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:17.633
what matters and then we
will disaggregate it later.

00:37:19.140 --> 00:37:20.033
First of all,

00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:24.150
let me say that pre-colonial Africa

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:29.563
might not have been a borderless world,

00:37:30.403 --> 00:37:32.090
this in the sense in which we have been

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:34.493
defining borders since yesterday.

00:37:36.580 --> 00:37:41.580
But where they existed,
borders were always porous

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:47.399
and permeable, that in fact

00:37:47.399 --> 00:37:51.803
the business of a border is to be crossed.

00:37:53.160 --> 00:37:56.170
That's what borders are for

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:00.463
but there is no border,

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:07.600
conceivable border,
outside of the principle,

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:09.403
the law of permeability.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.890
I can give you countless
practical examples

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:18.160
to instantiate this,

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:20.410
but just believe me for the time being

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:23.047
that's how it was.

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:30.390
As evidenced by traditions
of long distance trade

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:35.943
circulation was fundamental.

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:39.530
It was fundamental in the production of

00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:45.570
cultural forms, of political forms,

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:49.483
of economic and social
and religious forms.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:54.720
The most important vehicle for

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:57.100
transformation and change

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:01.853
was mobility.

00:39:02.850 --> 00:39:07.850
It was not class struggles in
the sense we understand it,

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:09.880
it was mobility.

00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:13.710
Mobility was the motor of any kind of

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:16.653
social or economic
political transformation.

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:22.930
In fact it was the driving principle

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:26.890
behind the delimitation and organization

00:39:26.890 --> 00:39:29.093
of space and territories.

00:39:30.710 --> 00:39:35.710
So the primordial principle
of spatial organization

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:40.030
was movement, was continuous movement

00:39:43.560 --> 00:39:47.790
and it is still part
of present-day culture.

00:39:52.090 --> 00:39:56.223
To stop is to run risks.

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:02.963
You have to be on the move constantly.

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:11.593
More and more, especially
in conditions of crisis,

00:40:14.960 --> 00:40:19.960
be on the move as a very
condition of your own survival

00:40:20.950 --> 00:40:23.350
because if you are not on the move

00:40:23.350 --> 00:40:26.593
the chances of survival are diminished.

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:36.160
So dominance, to dominate sovereignty

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:40.250
was not exclusively
expressed through the control

00:40:40.250 --> 00:40:44.600
of territory, physically
marked with borders.

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:45.910
It was not.

00:40:45.910 --> 00:40:47.840
Now you'll ask me but how was it then,

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:49.520
if you cannot control a territory,

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:51.503
how you can you exercise sovereignty?

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:56.260
How can you extract anything

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:58.893
since, as far as we know,

00:41:00.270 --> 00:41:05.110
power expresses itself,
manifests itself also

00:41:05.110 --> 00:41:09.190
if not primarily through
one or the other form

00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:10.833
of extraction of something.

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:19.610
All of that was expressed
through networks.

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:24.040
Networks, crossroads for those of you

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:27.900
who are familiar with African literature.

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:30.900
the importance of roads and crossroads

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:33.403
in African literature is astounding.

00:41:34.660 --> 00:41:39.503
Read Soyinka, read Chinua Achebe,

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:42.883
Amos Tutuola,

00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:46.020
roads and crossroads are everywhere

00:41:48.010 --> 00:41:49.323
in that literature.

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:55.580
So crossroads, flows of
people and flows of nature

00:41:56.990 --> 00:42:01.340
both in dialectical relationship

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:05.720
because in those cosmogonies

00:42:07.060 --> 00:42:09.610
people are unthinkable
without what we call nature.

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:15.480
So the realization, I mean the

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:18.203
Anthropocene term,

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:21.550
which seems to be a novelty

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:25.610
in parts of our world today,

00:42:25.610 --> 00:42:27.430
we have always lived in that.

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:28.263
It's not new

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:32.667
because you cannot think of people

00:42:32.667 --> 00:42:36.063
who are thinking of non-humans.

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:40.633
And here again, I mean read Tutuola,

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:45.210
it's a world of humans and non-humans

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:49.500
interacting, acting with others

00:42:51.000 --> 00:42:53.830
what Latour and others have called

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:55.153
this rigid agency,

00:42:57.220 --> 00:42:59.320
typical of those kinds

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:01.550
of let's say metaphysics

00:43:02.784 --> 00:43:05.053
of the human and non-human.

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:09.853
Now I don't want to exaggerate this.

00:43:11.430 --> 00:43:15.390
To be sure, fixed geographical

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:19.713
places such as towns
and villages did exist.

00:43:22.230 --> 00:43:23.540
People and things could

00:43:25.330 --> 00:43:27.810
be concentrated in a particular location

00:43:29.590 --> 00:43:31.970
and such places could even become

00:43:31.970 --> 00:43:35.360
the origin of movement
and there were links

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:39.530
between places, links such
as roads are mentioned then,

00:43:39.530 --> 00:43:40.603
flight paths,

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:46.103
but places were not described
by points or by lines.

00:43:47.700 --> 00:43:49.630
What mattered the most was

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:53.780
the distribution of
movement between places.

00:43:53.780 --> 00:43:56.080
Movement was the driving force

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:59.150
of the production of
space and movement itself

00:44:00.522 --> 00:44:02.827
if we were to believe
some of those cosmogonies

00:44:02.827 --> 00:44:04.170
you have in mind,

00:44:04.170 --> 00:44:06.832
the Dogon cosmogony that were

00:44:06.832 --> 00:44:10.703
particularly studied by Marcel Griaule,

00:44:12.370 --> 00:44:16.760
all other cosmogonies in equatorial Africa

00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:19.220
dealt with by anthropologists

00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:24.220
such as, and historians
such as Jan Vansina,

00:44:24.344 --> 00:44:27.453
Jansen and others,

00:44:28.420 --> 00:44:32.360
movement itself was not necessarily

00:44:32.360 --> 00:44:33.983
akin to displacement.

00:44:37.215 --> 00:44:40.200
What mattered the most
was the extent to which

00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:44.200
flows and the intensities intersected

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:46.630
and interacted with other flows,

00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:50.490
the new forms they could
take when they intensified.

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:53.770
So movement, especially among the Dogon,

00:44:53.770 --> 00:44:57.670
could lead to divergence,

00:44:57.670 --> 00:44:59.890
it could lead to convergence,

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:02.083
it could lead to intersections,

00:45:03.380 --> 00:45:04.850
and convergence, divergence

00:45:04.850 --> 00:45:09.850
and intersection were more
important than points.

00:45:12.450 --> 00:45:14.530
They were more important than lines,

00:45:14.530 --> 00:45:16.710
they were more important than surfaces,

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:19.400
lines, points and surfaces

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:23.973
as we know being cardinal reference in

00:45:25.020 --> 00:45:26.630
Western geometrics.

00:45:26.630 --> 00:45:27.840
So what we have here

00:45:29.346 --> 00:45:32.513
is, let's say, a different
kind of geometry.

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:38.938
It's an entirely
different kind of geometry

00:45:38.938 --> 00:45:42.700
out of which concepts of borders,

00:45:42.700 --> 00:45:45.840
concepts of power, concepts of relations,

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:48.713
concepts of separation derive.

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:54.630
So the point is, if we want to

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:56.940
harness

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:00.770
alternative resources,

00:46:00.770 --> 00:46:04.230
conceptual vocabulary type of that

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:08.293
to imagine a borderless
world here is an archive.

00:46:09.352 --> 00:46:10.780
I'm not saying that is the only one

00:46:12.210 --> 00:46:15.570
but what we have to
harness are the archives

00:46:15.570 --> 00:46:19.470
of the world at large and
not only the Western archive

00:46:19.470 --> 00:46:20.550
because in fact

00:46:23.480 --> 00:46:24.940
in the Western archive

00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:32.620
the Western archive
doesn't help us to develop

00:46:33.331 --> 00:46:35.663
an idea of borderlessness.

00:46:37.270 --> 00:46:40.450
The Western archive is premised on

00:46:40.450 --> 00:46:43.563
the crystallization of
the idea of the border,

00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:48.880
That we have to check later on,

00:46:48.880 --> 00:46:52.403
I'm just saying it as a provocation.

00:46:54.710 --> 00:46:56.150
So I could go on and on

00:46:57.275 --> 00:46:59.133
on these alternative geometries.

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:04.663
Let me just add one or two things.

00:47:07.610 --> 00:47:12.040
In this configuration, wealth and power

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:19.080
or let me say wealth in people

00:47:20.780 --> 00:47:24.750
always trumped wealth in things.

00:47:24.750 --> 00:47:25.973
Two forms of wealth,

00:47:27.871 --> 00:47:32.250
you could be wealthy in
terms of your capacity

00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:34.080
to agglomerate around you

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:40.400
clients, family members and so forth

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:43.540
and so on or you could be wealthy just by

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:47.810
virtue of having accumulated a huge

00:47:47.810 --> 00:47:51.070
amount of quantity of things.

00:47:51.070 --> 00:47:52.790
So you see here

00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:55.680
the nexus of quantities and qualities,

00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:58.693
qualities of persons,
categories of persons,

00:47:59.740 --> 00:48:02.740
being at play here

00:48:03.670 --> 00:48:08.670
and multiple forms of membership
were always available.

00:48:09.760 --> 00:48:12.520
You would be astonished,
you take one society

00:48:14.051 --> 00:48:18.950
and look into the dictionary
of forms of membership.

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:20.543
How is it that one belong?

00:48:21.806 --> 00:48:26.303
Through what window is it
that you can enter the house?

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:29.503
Multiple forms of membership,

00:48:30.510 --> 00:48:34.190
not a kind of rigid classification

00:48:34.190 --> 00:48:37.023
where either you are a citizen
or you are a foreigner.

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:42.770
In between being a citizen
and being a foreigner

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:47.410
there was a whole repertoire

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:51.183
of alternative forms of membership.

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:55.360
And building alliances through trade,

00:48:55.360 --> 00:48:59.610
marriage or religion,
incorporating newcomers,

00:48:59.610 --> 00:49:04.130
refugees, asylum seekers
into existing policies,

00:49:04.130 --> 00:49:05.293
that was the norm.

00:49:06.860 --> 00:49:10.433
You dominate by integrating foreigners,

00:49:13.606 --> 00:49:14.806
all kinds of foreigners,

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:20.460
and just to

00:49:27.260 --> 00:49:29.213
add to what I've just said,

00:49:31.362 --> 00:49:34.560
peoplehood, not talking about nationhood,

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:39.140
peaplehood included not only the living

00:49:39.140 --> 00:49:42.210
but also the dead and the unborn,

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:43.723
humans and non-humans.

00:49:45.548 --> 00:49:49.780
Exactly in line with all we are being told

00:49:49.780 --> 00:49:52.573
about in this age of the Anthropocene.

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:59.120
And community was unthinkable

00:49:59.240 --> 00:50:02.520
without some kind of foundational debt.

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:04.620
You know there are two, many,

00:50:04.620 --> 00:50:08.633
but two principal forms of debt.

00:50:09.520 --> 00:50:11.930
There is a kind of debt that is I would

00:50:11.930 --> 00:50:12.980
say it's proprietary.

00:50:14.420 --> 00:50:15.773
Kind of debt I mean banks,

00:50:18.055 --> 00:50:22.270
some of us are in debt to banks.

00:50:22.270 --> 00:50:23.673
Can you pay interest?

00:50:25.036 --> 00:50:28.440
If you don't pay interest,
they wake up one morning

00:50:29.825 --> 00:50:32.320
and the amount to pay for the interest has

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:35.900
overtaken the amount you
borrowed in the first instance.

00:50:35.900 --> 00:50:39.183
So that form of debt
are called proprietary.

00:50:40.470 --> 00:50:44.910
But in this constellation,

00:50:44.910 --> 00:50:47.520
human constellation talking about,

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:50.940
there's a different kind of
debt that is constitutive

00:50:52.390 --> 00:50:55.730
of their very basis of the relation

00:50:56.630 --> 00:51:00.700
and it's a kind of debt that encompasses

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:02.890
not only the living, the now,

00:51:02.890 --> 00:51:05.620
but also those who came before and those

00:51:05.620 --> 00:51:07.380
who will come after us,

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:11.130
that we have obligations
to that chain of beings

00:51:12.540 --> 00:51:15.980
which include, once again, not only humans

00:51:15.980 --> 00:51:19.320
but animals, but what we call nature

00:51:19.320 --> 00:51:20.933
and so forth and so on.

00:51:23.090 --> 00:51:26.199
Okay I can come back to that if you want

00:51:26.199 --> 00:51:31.199
to have further comments so
you see what I'm getting at.

00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:37.703
So those are the two models once again,

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:40.803
these are archetypes.

00:51:41.857 --> 00:51:43.280
They have to be properly historicized,

00:51:43.280 --> 00:51:45.800
what are the social forces driving what,

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:48.273
what are the limits we
all understand that.

00:51:55.200 --> 00:51:57.373
Where are we to today?

00:52:00.190 --> 00:52:01.480
It seems to me that

00:52:02.740 --> 00:52:05.690
worldwide, the combination of

00:52:06.650 --> 00:52:11.080
fast capitalism and we
mention it yesterday

00:52:11.080 --> 00:52:12.770
the saturation of the everyday

00:52:12.770 --> 00:52:16.813
by digital and computational technologies,

00:52:17.950 --> 00:52:19.600
these two things have led

00:52:21.083 --> 00:52:23.350
to the acceleration of speed

00:52:25.342 --> 00:52:27.292
and the intensification of connections.

00:52:28.500 --> 00:52:31.510
In fact the world is so connected today.

00:52:31.510 --> 00:52:34.847
Today basically you can sit where you are,

00:52:34.847 --> 00:52:37.800
you have access to your technologies

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:42.880
and you have access to
an amount of knowledge

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:44.623
we have never had access to.

00:52:48.280 --> 00:52:49.373
That is going on.

00:52:51.570 --> 00:52:54.950
at the same time willful ignorance is

00:52:56.366 --> 00:52:57.310
a feature of our times.

00:52:57.310 --> 00:53:00.390
how come we know so less when

00:53:00.390 --> 00:53:03.090
the amount of knowledge we have

00:53:03.090 --> 00:53:05.653
has never been as astounding as it is?

00:53:08.620 --> 00:53:12.230
Whatever the case, it
seems to me that ours

00:53:13.133 --> 00:53:16.420
is in this regard

00:53:16.420 --> 00:53:20.243
an era of planetary entanglement.

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:23.740
But this is not all,

00:53:26.408 --> 00:53:29.413
as Paul Virilio, Virilio
is a French scholar,

00:53:31.130 --> 00:53:34.553
but not only him countless
others have suggested,

00:53:36.110 --> 00:53:41.110
the twin phenomenon of
immediacy and instantaneity

00:53:43.667 --> 00:53:47.010
is presently one of the
most pressing problems

00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:49.550
confronting us or in any case

00:53:49.550 --> 00:53:52.760
one of the most determining factors

00:53:52.760 --> 00:53:56.113
ushering a new technolopolitical epoch.

00:53:57.333 --> 00:53:58.683
Since yesterday I have been

00:54:01.047 --> 00:54:02.740
trying to bring in discussion

00:54:04.483 --> 00:54:07.030
this event which is

00:54:07.030 --> 00:54:11.923
our emergence into a new technological age

00:54:14.054 --> 00:54:16.450
because we can't simply
understand what's going on

00:54:16.450 --> 00:54:20.863
if we don't factor in this
new technological development.

00:54:22.102 --> 00:54:23.870
So it seems to me that,

00:54:23.870 --> 00:54:26.293
in line with Virilio and the others,

00:54:27.154 --> 00:54:28.970
that one of the the most pressing

00:54:28.970 --> 00:54:30.433
problems confronting us,

00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:36.690
ushering us into this
new technopolitical epoch

00:54:36.690 --> 00:54:39.340
has to do, indeed, with the twin phenomena

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:43.123
of instantaneity and images.

00:54:45.220 --> 00:54:46.610
So in considering

00:54:47.580 --> 00:54:49.083
what borders have become,

00:54:50.940 --> 00:54:54.840
the kind of work they are doing

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:56.860
today, not yesterday, now

00:54:59.430 --> 00:55:03.350
or in considering what a borderless world

00:55:03.350 --> 00:55:05.810
or borderless Africa might look like

00:55:06.890 --> 00:55:09.510
it might be useful to take seriously

00:55:10.390 --> 00:55:11.790
the possibility that

00:55:13.540 --> 00:55:17.440
real time now prevails

00:55:17.440 --> 00:55:22.440
above both real space and
the geosphere in general.

00:55:24.060 --> 00:55:25.900
And that's really the key argument

00:55:25.900 --> 00:55:27.463
I would like to push tonight.

00:55:28.790 --> 00:55:33.763
The supersession of
real space by real time.

00:55:36.350 --> 00:55:39.250
Something that is made possible precisely

00:55:39.250 --> 00:55:40.083
because of

00:55:42.980 --> 00:55:45.400
this technopolitical event

00:55:45.400 --> 00:55:46.590
that is driven by

00:55:47.950 --> 00:55:50.573
all we have been referring
to since yesterday.

00:55:53.760 --> 00:55:56.420
The primacy of real time,

00:55:56.420 --> 00:55:57.810
of immediacy

00:56:00.280 --> 00:56:04.160
and instantaneity over and above

00:56:04.160 --> 00:56:08.110
space, over and above territory,

00:56:08.110 --> 00:56:10.153
over and above surface,

00:56:11.070 --> 00:56:13.330
might not yet be irreversible

00:56:13.330 --> 00:56:17.380
but it is in the process of becoming

00:56:17.380 --> 00:56:19.213
what we could call a fait accompli.

00:56:21.180 --> 00:56:23.520
Even if we wanted to reverse it,

00:56:23.520 --> 00:56:25.118
it might too late.

00:56:25.118 --> 00:56:27.363
That's what a fait accompli is.

00:56:29.735 --> 00:56:32.600
A fait accompli is also, by the way,

00:56:32.600 --> 00:56:34.917
that which nobody can account for.

00:56:36.380 --> 00:56:39.840
So question of unaccountability,

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:42.590
the impossibility to account for,

00:56:42.590 --> 00:56:45.920
or the difficulty to
account for which means

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:49.323
the difficulty to take responsibility for.

00:56:53.454 --> 00:56:57.240
In fact, of the three
main physical barriers

00:56:59.070 --> 00:57:02.520
science teaches us barrier of sound,

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:04.063
of heat and light,

00:57:05.190 --> 00:57:08.793
the first two have already been felled.

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:12.560
The sound barrier and
here I quote Virilio,

00:57:12.560 --> 00:57:15.607
has been, "cut across by the super

00:57:15.607 --> 00:57:19.287
"and hypersonic aircraft
while the heat barrier

00:57:19.287 --> 00:57:22.107
"is penetrated by the rockets taking

00:57:22.107 --> 00:57:25.487
"human beings outside the earth's orbit

00:57:25.487 --> 00:57:27.737
"in order to land them on the moon."

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:32.880
But the third barrier,
still Virilio speaking,

00:57:32.880 --> 00:57:36.477
that of light, "is not
something one can cross.

00:57:36.477 --> 00:57:37.773
"You crash into it.

00:57:38.738 --> 00:57:42.920
" It is precisely this barrier of time

00:57:42.920 --> 00:57:46.270
which confronts history in
the present day," he adds.

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:50.220
So building on Virilio's remarks

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:53.200
we can argue that the moment humanity

00:57:53.200 --> 00:57:55.250
will reach the light barrier,

00:57:55.250 --> 00:57:57.253
that is the speed of light,

00:57:58.355 --> 00:58:01.510
the historical event of
unprecedented importance

00:58:01.510 --> 00:58:02.343
will happen,

00:58:03.200 --> 00:58:07.200
which might throw history
itself in disarray

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:10.800
and might jumble up the
relation of the living beings

00:58:10.800 --> 00:58:11.983
towards the world.

00:58:13.942 --> 00:58:14.775
What do I mean by that?

00:58:14.775 --> 00:58:17.270
By that I mean, when it happens

00:58:17.270 --> 00:58:20.273
then we will have entered
a borderless world.

00:58:21.440 --> 00:58:24.650
We'll enter a borderless world when,

00:58:24.650 --> 00:58:26.103
let me put it differently,

00:58:32.361 --> 00:58:36.357
when humanity reaches the light barrier

00:58:38.152 --> 00:58:38.985
because territory

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:42.632
it won't vanish, it will still be there

00:58:42.632 --> 00:58:43.883
but it will become irrelevant.

00:58:45.490 --> 00:58:46.790
It'll become irrelevant

00:58:48.260 --> 00:58:49.643
because precisely,

00:58:52.360 --> 00:58:56.320
we will have finally
domesticated absolute speed

00:58:56.320 --> 00:58:57.383
and having done so,

00:58:58.340 --> 00:59:01.833
the earth will enter a new
period of its deep history,

00:59:02.810 --> 00:59:04.920
one in which real time will

00:59:05.830 --> 00:59:08.933
really have superseded real space.

00:59:09.930 --> 00:59:12.973
So let me just say that
we are not yet there.

00:59:14.756 --> 00:59:18.483
We might not get there in our lifetime,

00:59:19.500 --> 00:59:20.973
some of us at least,

00:59:23.557 --> 00:59:24.940
but take for instance

00:59:24.940 --> 00:59:26.490
algorithmic trading,

00:59:26.490 --> 00:59:29.610
also known as high-frequency trading.

00:59:29.610 --> 00:59:32.783
Computerized, fully automated,

00:59:34.153 --> 00:59:38.620
it is obvious that financial markets are

00:59:38.620 --> 00:59:43.620
but one dimension of the ecology
of high-speed materiality

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:48.120
that is transforming not only
the nature of market access

00:59:48.120 --> 00:59:52.020
in our time, but also, I should add,

00:59:52.020 --> 00:59:53.793
the very nature of borders.

00:59:56.180 --> 00:59:58.820
And in that sense it's not simply a

00:59:58.820 --> 01:00:00.293
technological development.

01:00:04.690 --> 01:00:09.614
So what I'm arguing for is that in order

01:00:09.614 --> 01:00:11.064
to imagine a borderless world

01:00:12.080 --> 01:00:15.130
we have to go beyond the liberal idea

01:00:15.130 --> 01:00:17.533
of movement and speed and,

01:00:19.710 --> 01:00:24.174
somewhat, draw from other archives.

01:00:24.174 --> 01:00:26.080
I'll end all of this now by

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:28.320
putting on the table

01:00:30.134 --> 01:00:33.073
a notion I take from the
Ghanaian constitution.

01:00:34.350 --> 01:00:36.420
The Ghanaian constitution of Ghana

01:00:37.950 --> 01:00:41.870
has developed a concept I haven't found

01:00:41.870 --> 01:00:42.713
anywhere else.

01:00:44.180 --> 01:00:48.450
It is the concept, a new right,

01:00:48.450 --> 01:00:53.450
they call the right of a abode, A-B-O-D-E

01:00:56.586 --> 01:00:58.370
as a fundamental right, that is

01:00:59.960 --> 01:01:03.563
they want to add to the list
of traditional human rights.

01:01:07.110 --> 01:01:10.220
It seems to me that this idea

01:01:10.220 --> 01:01:12.130
of the right of abode

01:01:15.180 --> 01:01:20.180
is a cornerstone for any real imagination

01:01:21.710 --> 01:01:24.283
of Africa as a borderless space.

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:32.290
I say this because

01:01:35.560 --> 01:01:39.940
if Africa is to contribute to

01:01:41.375 --> 01:01:43.520
the unfolding global
debate on human mobility

01:01:43.520 --> 01:01:46.090
in this age of territory entitlement

01:01:50.933 --> 01:01:51.873
it needs to indeed

01:01:58.570 --> 01:02:01.143
dig deeply into its own archives.

01:02:02.840 --> 01:02:05.978
In a world in which arrest, detention

01:02:05.978 --> 01:02:08.270
and incarceration, including at sea,

01:02:08.270 --> 01:02:13.270
abandonment, deportation are seen as

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:16.860
the most efficient ways of dealing with

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:21.050
economic migrants,
refugees, asylum seekers

01:02:22.110 --> 01:02:25.860
we should draw from our
own historical perspective,

01:02:25.860 --> 01:02:30.040
experience as African and diasporic people

01:02:31.450 --> 01:02:36.280
an articulate proposal for
ourselves and for the world

01:02:38.317 --> 01:02:41.360
which is radically different
from the anti-humanist norms

01:02:41.360 --> 01:02:45.260
and practices currently
prevalent on matters

01:02:45.260 --> 01:02:49.763
concerning marginal human
mobility in the world.

01:02:51.600 --> 01:02:54.110
I say this because at
the deep historical level

01:02:55.400 --> 01:02:58.920
African diasporic struggles for freedom

01:02:58.920 --> 01:03:02.840
and self-determination have always been

01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:07.840
intertwined with the
aspiration to move and change,

01:03:11.550 --> 01:03:14.033
whether under conditions of slavery,

01:03:15.450 --> 01:03:18.020
Atlantic slavery, Trans-Saharan slavery,

01:03:18.020 --> 01:03:19.500
under colonial rule

01:03:20.520 --> 01:03:24.830
the loss of our sovereignty
automatically resulted

01:03:24.830 --> 01:03:27.973
in the loss of our right to free movement.

01:03:30.290 --> 01:03:31.480
This is the reason why

01:03:33.060 --> 01:03:37.693
the dream of a free redeemed
and multi African nation,

01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:41.260
what Marcus Garvey called

01:03:42.120 --> 01:03:45.993
a bright star among the
constellation of nations,

01:03:47.140 --> 01:03:49.780
this is why this dream has been

01:03:49.780 --> 01:03:53.980
inextricably linked to
the recovery of the right

01:03:53.980 --> 01:03:57.550
to come and go without let or hindrance

01:03:58.460 --> 01:04:01.053
across our colossal continent.

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:06.060
In fact, our history in modernity

01:04:06.060 --> 01:04:08.420
has been to a large extent

01:04:08.420 --> 01:04:12.740
one of constant displacement
and confinement,

01:04:12.740 --> 01:04:15.713
forced migrations and coerced labor.

01:04:16.680 --> 01:04:19.440
Think of the plantation system

01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:21.733
in the Americas and the Caribbean.

01:04:22.670 --> 01:04:27.250
Think of the black codes,
the pig laws or the

01:04:27.250 --> 01:04:30.250
vagrancy status after the failure

01:04:30.250 --> 01:04:34.853
of reconstruction in the
United States in 1887.

01:04:35.880 --> 01:04:39.980
Think of the chain gangs laboring attached

01:04:39.980 --> 01:04:44.110
such as road construction, ditch digging,

01:04:44.110 --> 01:04:48.063
tearing and deforestation
and so forth and so on.

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:53.390
Think too of the Code de L'Indigenat

01:04:53.390 --> 01:04:55.610
in French colonial Africa,

01:04:55.610 --> 01:04:59.890
of the Bantustans and labor
reserves in southern Africa

01:05:00.980 --> 01:05:04.130
and of the Kaesong Industrial Complex in

01:05:04.130 --> 01:05:06.363
today's United States of America.

01:05:07.650 --> 01:05:11.431
In each instance, to be African

01:05:11.431 --> 01:05:16.431
and to be black has meant to be consigned

01:05:16.460 --> 01:05:20.790
to one or the other of the
many spaces of confinement

01:05:20.790 --> 01:05:22.303
modernity invented.

01:05:23.810 --> 01:05:26.490
The scramble for Africa
in the 19th century

01:05:27.380 --> 01:05:31.810
and the carving of its
boundaries along colonial lines

01:05:31.810 --> 01:05:35.640
turned the continent into
a massive castle space

01:05:36.920 --> 01:05:40.030
and each of us into a potential

01:05:40.030 --> 01:05:44.180
illegal migrant, unable to move except

01:05:44.180 --> 01:05:47.573
under increasingly punitive conditions.

01:05:48.750 --> 01:05:52.300
As a matter of fact, entrapment became

01:05:52.300 --> 01:05:55.470
the precondition for the exploitation of

01:05:55.470 --> 01:05:58.730
our labor which is why the struggles

01:05:58.730 --> 01:06:02.590
for emancipation and
racial upliftment were

01:06:02.590 --> 01:06:05.740
so intertwined with the struggles for

01:06:05.740 --> 01:06:07.493
the right to move freely.

01:06:12.190 --> 01:06:13.990
So if we want to

01:06:16.120 --> 01:06:19.630
conclude the work of decolonization

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:25.370
we have to bring down colonial
boundaries in our continent

01:06:25.370 --> 01:06:28.950
and turn Africa into a
vast space of circulation

01:06:30.410 --> 01:06:34.270
for itself, for its descendants

01:06:34.270 --> 01:06:37.160
and for anyone who wants
to tie his or her fate

01:06:37.160 --> 01:06:38.780
with our continent.

01:06:38.780 --> 01:06:41.360
That's the importance of the concept

01:06:41.360 --> 01:06:44.020
of the right of abode

01:06:44.020 --> 01:06:47.020
as written in the Ghanaian constitution.

01:06:47.020 --> 01:06:48.257
Thank you very much.

01:06:49.363 --> 01:06:52.446
(audience applauds)

01:07:03.959 --> 01:07:06.372
- [Man] We have time for some questions.

01:07:06.372 --> 01:07:08.726
If you have a question
please go to the mic

01:07:08.726 --> 01:07:12.059
on your side (mumbles).

01:07:25.840 --> 01:07:28.980
- [Audience member] So you
talk eloquently about the

01:07:28.980 --> 01:07:33.260
importance of movement without visas

01:07:33.260 --> 01:07:36.290
and without, you know, any
kind of application process

01:07:36.290 --> 01:07:39.260
and it's very moving to
think of it in those terms,

01:07:39.260 --> 01:07:43.600
you know in terms of the
traditional constitution of Africa

01:07:43.600 --> 01:07:46.280
as the casual space but at the same time

01:07:46.280 --> 01:07:48.350
you also talk about the importance of

01:07:48.350 --> 01:07:51.460
indebtedness, that we're indebted

01:07:51.460 --> 01:07:56.040
to the debt and to some extent we're also

01:07:56.040 --> 01:07:59.926
indebted or we can be held answerable

01:07:59.926 --> 01:08:03.923
to to the future, to our descendants.

01:08:04.770 --> 01:08:07.390
It seems to me that the two actually point

01:08:07.390 --> 01:08:09.170
in slightly different directions

01:08:10.270 --> 01:08:13.190
in the sense that being
indebted or acknowledging

01:08:13.190 --> 01:08:17.579
that we are, that
somebody else will hold us

01:08:17.579 --> 01:08:20.320
in their debt, whatever,

01:08:20.320 --> 01:08:21.930
that one form or another,

01:08:21.930 --> 01:08:24.277
either forward looking or backward looking

01:08:24.277 --> 01:08:28.500
that puts us in a kind of a sequel

01:08:28.500 --> 01:08:30.480
in which we do occupy a space

01:08:30.480 --> 01:08:35.480
that cannot be freely relinquished.

01:08:35.640 --> 01:08:37.750
So I just wonder if you could

01:08:37.750 --> 01:08:39.150
comment a little bit about

01:08:40.320 --> 01:08:44.934
the sense of unbounded
mobility on the one hand

01:08:44.934 --> 01:08:48.300
and the sense of being obligated and being

01:08:48.300 --> 01:08:50.790
held by our obligation,

01:08:50.790 --> 01:08:53.373
which implies a certain
kind of constraint.

01:08:57.650 --> 01:09:00.820
- Should we take a few questions, Gary?

01:09:00.820 --> 01:09:02.643
Okay, take two.

01:09:05.690 --> 01:09:07.980
- [Audience member] So my
question pertains a little bit

01:09:07.980 --> 01:09:09.873
to the porous borders.

01:09:10.880 --> 01:09:12.800
In some cases it does seem that

01:09:12.800 --> 01:09:15.420
there are regimes or
arrangements where borders

01:09:15.420 --> 01:09:19.520
are becoming more porous
but the exchange is

01:09:19.520 --> 01:09:21.270
extreme surveillance and monitoring

01:09:22.680 --> 01:09:24.140
and I can think of a few cases where

01:09:24.140 --> 01:09:28.290
it's relatively easy to
enter various spaces,

01:09:28.290 --> 01:09:31.622
countries, but the
exchange is that there's

01:09:31.622 --> 01:09:34.500
better biometric information, as you

01:09:34.500 --> 01:09:36.660
eluded to but also better surveillance,

01:09:36.660 --> 01:09:39.080
whether it's monitoring
you via your phone,

01:09:39.080 --> 01:09:40.580
via satellite, via drone

01:09:41.730 --> 01:09:44.050
and I wonder if perhaps
one of the directions

01:09:44.050 --> 01:09:46.760
that some of these
arrangements might be going in

01:09:47.599 --> 01:09:48.800
is you can go wherever you want

01:09:48.800 --> 01:09:50.820
but we're going to know
exactly what you're doing,

01:09:50.820 --> 01:09:52.873
where you are, who you're meeting with.

01:09:53.863 --> 01:09:55.670
So there's a freedom of
movement but there's no longer

01:09:55.670 --> 01:09:59.773
really a freedom to anonymity
or to privacy, thanks.

01:10:13.230 --> 01:10:14.063
- See I was,

01:10:22.065 --> 01:10:24.750
I have my own convictions on each of

01:10:24.750 --> 01:10:27.143
issues I put on the table.

01:10:28.323 --> 01:10:30.953
I was, I can talk about that.

01:10:32.270 --> 01:10:33.970
I was trying to

01:10:36.710 --> 01:10:39.630
offer a way

01:10:40.710 --> 01:10:45.610
in which we can recapture in these

01:10:45.610 --> 01:10:49.483
times of utopian atrophy,

01:10:50.550 --> 01:10:53.780
we can recapture the utopian resources

01:10:53.780 --> 01:10:57.950
that are embedded in the idea of a world

01:10:57.950 --> 01:10:59.450
that wouldn't have any border.

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:04.430
In particular a continent, for the reasons

01:11:04.430 --> 01:11:05.993
I mentioned at the end,

01:11:06.860 --> 01:11:09.710
that would become

01:11:09.710 --> 01:11:12.920
really free for its people and

01:11:14.290 --> 01:11:17.383
would speak to the world
from that position.

01:11:21.977 --> 01:11:23.320
So that's the framework

01:11:23.320 --> 01:11:25.433
I was trying to to build.

01:11:28.451 --> 01:11:29.284
And of course,

01:11:32.050 --> 01:11:34.830
as far as unbounded mobility is concerned

01:11:36.253 --> 01:11:37.890
I think that it's a project we

01:11:37.890 --> 01:11:40.280
have to take seriously.

01:11:40.280 --> 01:11:41.820
We have to take it seriously

01:11:44.161 --> 01:11:46.700
and whether or not it is

01:11:46.700 --> 01:11:47.803
actually feasible.

01:11:51.524 --> 01:11:53.370
In fact we have to hold dear onto it

01:11:53.370 --> 01:11:56.850
precisely at the moment when it appears to

01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:59.023
be totally utopian.

01:12:01.907 --> 01:12:03.770
I argue in that way

01:12:07.020 --> 01:12:08.853
for all kinds of reasons,

01:12:10.010 --> 01:12:12.240
one of which has to do

01:12:12.240 --> 01:12:15.670
with what I really believe is

01:12:16.620 --> 01:12:18.967
our entry into a planetary age.

01:12:20.572 --> 01:12:23.700
And this doesn't mean that
nation states don't exist

01:12:23.700 --> 01:12:25.493
any longer, they are still there,

01:12:26.660 --> 01:12:30.210
I'm not subscribing to the kind of

01:12:31.873 --> 01:12:33.890
discourse of happy
globalization we witnessed

01:12:33.890 --> 01:12:36.380
in the 90s when many believed

01:12:36.380 --> 01:12:39.050
that in fact nation states

01:12:39.050 --> 01:12:40.490
were going to disappear.

01:12:40.490 --> 01:12:42.130
If anything they have

01:12:42.130 --> 01:12:45.050
shown that they are extremely resilient

01:12:45.050 --> 01:12:50.050
and they can mutate with the times.

01:12:50.610 --> 01:12:53.610
So I have to hold on on to that

01:12:57.692 --> 01:13:01.490
precisely because in
spite of their existence

01:13:03.300 --> 01:13:07.240
none of the issues we
humanity is dealing with today

01:13:07.240 --> 01:13:09.473
can result at a national level alone.

01:13:12.050 --> 01:13:15.510
So I put forward this

01:13:15.510 --> 01:13:20.080
renewal idea in the hope that

01:13:23.600 --> 01:13:27.300
this renewed planetary consciousness

01:13:27.300 --> 01:13:28.740
might force us to

01:13:30.740 --> 01:13:33.720
address the questions I was

01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:35.710
putting forward today,

01:13:35.710 --> 01:13:37.193
whose earth is this?

01:13:38.844 --> 01:13:40.303
Rather than whose country is this?

01:13:43.220 --> 01:13:46.700
How do we distribute it, meaning not how

01:13:46.700 --> 01:13:48.760
we conquer it for ourselves but meaning

01:13:48.760 --> 01:13:50.043
how do we share it?

01:13:51.070 --> 01:13:53.030
How do we share it

01:13:53.030 --> 01:13:56.220
because sharing it is the very condition

01:13:56.220 --> 01:13:57.693
of its sustainability?

01:13:59.932 --> 01:14:00.765
At the moment when

01:14:01.749 --> 01:14:04.170
the possibility of extinction

01:14:05.640 --> 01:14:08.850
is real, it is plausible,

01:14:08.850 --> 01:14:11.080
it's not implausible

01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:18.030
so the question of
inhabitation of the world

01:14:18.030 --> 01:14:19.803
at large, of the earth,

01:14:20.820 --> 01:14:24.470
the question of sharing it as a condition

01:14:24.470 --> 01:14:26.430
for its own durability

01:14:27.790 --> 01:14:31.230
those questions become extremely important

01:14:31.230 --> 01:14:33.100
especially if we want to move beyond

01:14:33.100 --> 01:14:35.530
just an ethnographic description

01:14:35.530 --> 01:14:38.220
of how borders work

01:14:38.220 --> 01:14:40.823
and the kind of work they accomplish.

01:14:42.051 --> 01:14:44.120
So if we want, the project is to inject

01:14:45.180 --> 01:14:49.230
contemporary debates on
migration, movement, mobility

01:14:49.230 --> 01:14:52.810
with the kind of
philosophical and theoretical

01:14:52.810 --> 01:14:57.800
urgency that is made available to us

01:14:57.800 --> 01:14:59.750
with the realization that we have

01:14:59.750 --> 01:15:03.160
entered techno planetary age.

01:15:03.160 --> 01:15:06.170
And then the questions of unboundedness

01:15:06.170 --> 01:15:10.830
and obligation to locality it seems to me

01:15:10.830 --> 01:15:14.063
they can be reshaped differently.

01:15:16.260 --> 01:15:19.330
That would be a way of let's say

01:15:19.330 --> 01:15:22.533
commenting on your comments.

01:15:27.410 --> 01:15:29.901
You see on the one hand

01:15:29.901 --> 01:15:31.310
if I limit myself to the continent

01:15:32.250 --> 01:15:33.700
what we are really witnessing

01:15:34.899 --> 01:15:37.340
is a double process, one of closure,

01:15:37.340 --> 01:15:40.520
the other one of an
acceleration in terms of

01:15:40.520 --> 01:15:42.793
opening of African borders.

01:15:43.649 --> 01:15:46.480
Now there are 27
countries in the continent

01:15:46.480 --> 01:15:50.280
where if you have an African passport

01:15:50.280 --> 01:15:53.340
you can obtain your visa at arrival.

01:15:53.340 --> 01:15:56.113
That wasn't the case
two or three years ago.

01:15:57.285 --> 01:15:59.643
It's now the case.

01:16:00.510 --> 01:16:03.480
Last week they signed in Kigali

01:16:03.480 --> 01:16:04.893
a new treaty,

01:16:06.010 --> 01:16:08.170
same kind of treaties you see everywhere,

01:16:08.170 --> 01:16:09.720
NAFTA and the rest,

01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:13.110
so they are moving in those directions,

01:16:13.110 --> 01:16:16.793
want a free market for
the whole continent.

01:16:18.220 --> 01:16:19.690
But as I was suggesting

01:16:21.252 --> 01:16:23.773
it seems to me that the

01:16:26.260 --> 01:16:30.020
most plausible way probably to

01:16:32.899 --> 01:16:35.270
dismantle national
borders in the continent

01:16:37.060 --> 01:16:40.010
might have to go through

01:16:40.010 --> 01:16:42.853
domestication of new technologies.

01:16:44.089 --> 01:16:45.139
I really believe that

01:16:47.390 --> 01:16:50.780
paradoxically not that technologies

01:16:50.780 --> 01:16:53.130
are neutral but

01:16:53.130 --> 01:16:57.190
they are the the most direct way

01:16:57.190 --> 01:17:01.000
in which colonial boundaries
in Africa will fall.

01:17:01.000 --> 01:17:03.160
I can give you plenty of examples of

01:17:03.160 --> 01:17:05.330
how it is already happening.

01:17:05.330 --> 01:17:08.300
So on the one hand borders are becoming

01:17:08.300 --> 01:17:10.440
a bit more porous than before

01:17:11.545 --> 01:17:13.495
but on the other hand there is a global

01:17:15.220 --> 01:17:20.220
drive to turn the continent into

01:17:20.254 --> 01:17:22.560
a castle space.

01:17:22.560 --> 01:17:24.040
Very few Africans

01:17:28.840 --> 01:17:32.117
are welcomed as migrants
anywhere in the world.

01:17:33.070 --> 01:17:36.070
You give me one example of

01:17:36.070 --> 01:17:38.940
Africans arriving in any part of the world

01:17:38.940 --> 01:17:40.483
where they are welcomed.

01:17:43.110 --> 01:17:47.520
Europe in its attempt at reinforcing

01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:48.920
its southern boundaries

01:17:49.810 --> 01:17:53.830
is actively recruiting

01:17:53.830 --> 01:17:57.460
the number of transit countries

01:17:58.646 --> 01:18:01.630
in making sure that its
borders are extended

01:18:03.140 --> 01:18:04.360
in the continent itself

01:18:05.770 --> 01:18:10.410
but you can't have a situation
where people cannot go out

01:18:10.410 --> 01:18:12.313
and they cannot circulate from within.

01:18:13.370 --> 01:18:17.320
That double penalty is, it seems to me,

01:18:17.320 --> 01:18:18.910
one of the biggest threats to

01:18:21.580 --> 01:18:24.713
movement of people right now.

01:18:36.472 --> 01:18:39.194
- [Audience member] I was
just listening to you today

01:18:39.194 --> 01:18:41.230
and yesterday I was thinking about,

01:18:41.230 --> 01:18:44.510
well I was reminded of one of Derrida's

01:18:44.510 --> 01:18:46.780
deep frustration with Kant

01:18:46.780 --> 01:18:49.230
and his frustration was that Kant

01:18:49.230 --> 01:18:52.460
restricted his concept of cosmopolitanism

01:18:52.460 --> 01:18:54.670
to the right of visitation and hardly ever

01:18:54.670 --> 01:18:56.340
to the right of residence

01:18:56.340 --> 01:18:59.160
and it seems to me that what is central

01:18:59.160 --> 01:19:02.110
to your talk, what was
central to your talk today

01:19:02.110 --> 01:19:04.830
and your talk yesterday

01:19:04.830 --> 01:19:08.530
was the idea of moving freely

01:19:08.530 --> 01:19:09.830
and I wonder if you could

01:19:09.830 --> 01:19:12.340
perhaps talk a bit more about

01:19:12.340 --> 01:19:17.333
the twin question of settling freely.

01:19:18.347 --> 01:19:22.713
The right to settle freely
as opposed to move freely.

01:19:25.160 --> 01:19:28.740
Settle, settle, settle, settle

01:19:28.740 --> 01:19:31.120
Residence
- Oh residence

01:19:31.120 --> 01:19:33.590
- As opposed to (laughs)

01:19:33.590 --> 01:19:34.423
visit.

01:19:35.371 --> 01:19:36.204
- Oh okay.

01:19:37.244 --> 01:19:39.653
Okay, okay I see.

01:19:41.430 --> 01:19:44.313
So settling as opposed to moving.

01:19:48.700 --> 01:19:51.840
Frankly I don't know why I didn't

01:19:51.840 --> 01:19:52.800
pay attention to

01:19:54.500 --> 01:19:55.363
to settling.

01:20:06.360 --> 01:20:07.193
Maybe because

01:20:11.329 --> 01:20:12.162
of the antecedent

01:20:13.179 --> 01:20:14.030
sedentary bias

01:20:17.530 --> 01:20:18.890
that is my own bias

01:20:19.990 --> 01:20:22.382
but I have a feeling that I mean

01:20:22.382 --> 01:20:24.073
what we are all encouraged to do today

01:20:24.073 --> 01:20:24.906
is just to settle.

01:20:26.890 --> 01:20:28.810
Stay where, especially

01:20:28.810 --> 01:20:29.883
stay where you are.

01:20:31.920 --> 01:20:34.040
Just stay where you are, don't move around

01:20:35.810 --> 01:20:38.993
and therefore I was not
interested in that part.

01:20:43.195 --> 01:20:44.545
I haven't thought about it.

01:20:50.900 --> 01:20:52.900
I don't think that is the problem today.

01:20:56.770 --> 01:20:58.586
I don't know what do you think

01:20:58.586 --> 01:21:00.457
but it seems to me that

01:21:00.457 --> 01:21:01.920
we are all encouraged
to stay where we are,

01:21:01.920 --> 01:21:03.293
not travel too much,

01:21:04.900 --> 01:21:06.800
not settle as well

01:21:06.800 --> 01:21:09.263
just be where we belong.

01:21:11.270 --> 01:21:13.720
So the best way to shake that is to

01:21:13.720 --> 01:21:16.520
look into the opposite which is

01:21:16.520 --> 01:21:17.420
how do we make sure

01:21:17.420 --> 01:21:18.743
that we move?

01:21:19.840 --> 01:21:21.530
How do we make sure that

01:21:21.530 --> 01:21:25.337
settlement is not the same as confinement

01:21:25.337 --> 01:21:26.930
which it has been,

01:21:26.930 --> 01:21:30.770
meaning some people's histories

01:21:31.778 --> 01:21:33.710
in the plantation,

01:21:33.710 --> 01:21:37.193
you quote unquote settle

01:21:41.480 --> 01:21:43.820
and if you move

01:21:43.820 --> 01:21:46.440
then it's an attempt,

01:21:46.440 --> 01:21:48.033
it's a crime against property.

01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:54.443
So I haven't thought about
it but I will think about it.

01:21:55.430 --> 01:21:56.263
Yes.

01:21:57.100 --> 01:21:59.275
- [Audience member] I
wanna to say thank you,

01:21:59.275 --> 01:22:01.221
you've given us so much to think about

01:22:01.221 --> 01:22:03.490
and powerful images for

01:22:03.490 --> 01:22:07.270
thinking differently
about the world we're in.

01:22:07.270 --> 01:22:10.060
Your account of Africa resonated very much

01:22:10.060 --> 01:22:13.170
with our thinking about the Americas

01:22:13.170 --> 01:22:17.170
where migrants are clamoring

01:22:17.170 --> 01:22:19.880
for the right not to migrate as well as

01:22:19.880 --> 01:22:22.700
the right to migrate and they link

01:22:22.700 --> 01:22:25.030
the protection of mobility

01:22:25.030 --> 01:22:27.770
to the possibility of restoration of home

01:22:28.940 --> 01:22:32.490
and are also asking for something besides

01:22:32.490 --> 01:22:34.420
the path to citizenship

01:22:34.420 --> 01:22:38.200
as the means to achieve
and redeem themselves as

01:22:39.370 --> 01:22:42.133
persons with protection and legal status.

01:22:44.751 --> 01:22:46.330
So your critique of the
liberal framework about

01:22:46.330 --> 01:22:50.400
freedom and security is very well taken.

01:22:50.400 --> 01:22:54.950
My question is for the economics

01:22:54.950 --> 01:22:57.173
a bit within this technology.

01:22:58.640 --> 01:23:00.660
My work, recently I've been looking at

01:23:01.686 --> 01:23:03.520
the use of x-ray

01:23:03.520 --> 01:23:05.270
and the tracking and I think it goes to

01:23:05.270 --> 01:23:07.610
this question about settling,

01:23:07.610 --> 01:23:12.610
is that there is a category of people

01:23:13.090 --> 01:23:16.650
who in the imagination
of this they are not

01:23:16.650 --> 01:23:19.630
meant to live socially

01:23:19.630 --> 01:23:21.430
right and in relation

01:23:21.430 --> 01:23:24.590
but to be the surplus

01:23:24.590 --> 01:23:26.800
who can be brought and left

01:23:26.800 --> 01:23:30.071
so that along with immobility

01:23:30.071 --> 01:23:33.040
is the forced migration and
the guest worker program

01:23:33.040 --> 01:23:36.810
and the category of
existence in which they live

01:23:36.810 --> 01:23:39.810
so long away from home

01:23:39.810 --> 01:23:43.300
that they no longer have
that capacity of social

01:23:44.307 --> 01:23:47.480
and relation or where

01:23:47.480 --> 01:23:51.125
because they're incarcerated
the future growth

01:23:51.125 --> 01:23:52.834
of their population in the U.S.

01:23:52.834 --> 01:23:53.934
will be limited right,

01:23:54.815 --> 01:23:56.940
where there's a bio-political
project around race.

01:23:56.940 --> 01:24:01.180
But the work that I've
been doing with x-ray,

01:24:01.180 --> 01:24:05.090
and I wanted to hear you
talk more about the body,

01:24:05.090 --> 01:24:07.110
is an optic of state control

01:24:07.110 --> 01:24:08.830
of looking inside the body

01:24:10.060 --> 01:24:14.920
that is doing something different

01:24:14.920 --> 01:24:18.900
from, it seems to me,
other regimes of race

01:24:18.900 --> 01:24:20.393
and racial profiling.

01:24:21.670 --> 01:24:23.940
You've said very powerfully

01:24:23.940 --> 01:24:25.850
the way it travels with the person and

01:24:25.850 --> 01:24:28.110
becomes part of the person's biography

01:24:28.110 --> 01:24:30.810
and works against the
capacity of the person

01:24:30.810 --> 01:24:32.820
to represent themselves

01:24:33.670 --> 01:24:38.460
but it also, it seems
to me, is really again

01:24:38.460 --> 01:24:40.900
linked to this production of an ideal

01:24:42.300 --> 01:24:45.240
non-person who can be

01:24:47.500 --> 01:24:50.950
made into a unit of work,

01:24:50.950 --> 01:24:52.880
a unit of value

01:24:52.880 --> 01:24:57.380
even as they're represented
in political discourse

01:24:57.380 --> 01:25:00.450
as the refuse of nations,
as people who are

01:25:00.450 --> 01:25:02.950
in their improper place or who are

01:25:02.950 --> 01:25:05.050
surplus, do you know what I mean?

01:25:05.050 --> 01:25:07.530
So there's the discourse that says

01:25:07.530 --> 01:25:10.030
these are the surplus people, right,

01:25:10.030 --> 01:25:13.540
who are improperly here

01:25:14.870 --> 01:25:18.120
but they're being produced as non-persons

01:25:18.120 --> 01:25:23.120
in the way statelessness
for an economic function

01:25:23.139 --> 01:25:25.530
in ways that I'd love to hear

01:25:25.530 --> 01:25:28.360
you maybe think about with me

01:25:28.360 --> 01:25:31.150
because I hear very much
the the beautiful work

01:25:31.150 --> 01:25:33.280
that you've done around the political

01:25:33.280 --> 01:25:35.800
order of things I just wanted to ask you

01:25:35.800 --> 01:25:39.640
to think the economic logic of that

01:25:40.770 --> 01:25:43.990
border space a little bit more if you

01:25:43.990 --> 01:25:45.730
get my question.

01:25:45.730 --> 01:25:47.250
I have too many questions at once

01:25:47.250 --> 01:25:48.800
I think is my problem (laughs).

01:25:51.937 --> 01:25:54.430
- We can deal with some of them tomorrow.

01:25:57.130 --> 01:26:00.483
Looking at Gary and Peter and the clock.

01:26:07.440 --> 01:26:10.357
(audience mumbles)

01:26:17.959 --> 01:26:20.959
(audience applauds)

01:26:26.243 --> 01:26:28.910
(gentle music)

